# Logging: use emet.utils.logger for colored output

Use `emet.utils.logger.Logger` instead of `logging.getLogger()` so that
warnings print in yellow and errors print in red on the terminal.

## Usage

```python
from emet.utils.logger import Logger

logger = Logger(__name__)

logger.error("Something broke")       # red, stderr
logger.warning("Falling back to X")   # yellow, stderr
logger.info("Loaded model")           # white, stdout
logger.alert("Ready")                 # green, stdout
logger.debug("Step details")          # white, stdout (hidden by default)
```

## Do NOT use

```python
import logging
logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)   # no color, easy to miss warnings
```

## Formatting

The custom Logger joins `*args` with spaces. Use f-strings, not `%s`:

```python
# Good
logger.warning(f"SAM3 not available ({err}), falling back")

# Bad — %s formatting is for stdlib logging only
logger.warning("SAM3 not available (%s), falling back", err)
```

## Module-level shortcuts

For one-off calls without a named logger:

```python
from emet.utils.logger import warning, error
warning("Feature X unavailable")
```
